If you spend any time online,

3
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:05,694
you've seen the videos.

4
00:00:05,874 --> 00:00:08,453
Buy a vending machine and make passive

5
00:00:08,453 --> 00:00:08,928
income.

6
00:00:09,108 --> 00:00:11,058
Own a car wash and print money.

7
00:00:11,238 --> 00:00:14,349
Storage units are the best investment nobody

8
00:00:14,349 --> 00:00:15,197
talks about.

9
00:00:15,377 --> 00:00:16,863
Some of that is true.

10
00:00:17,043 --> 00:00:18,438
A lot of it leaves out the

11
00:00:18,438 --> 00:00:19,029
hard parts.

12
00:00:19,209 --> 00:00:21,692
Today, we're going to run the real

13
00:00:21,692 --> 00:00:23,226
numbers on all three,

14
00:00:23,226 --> 00:00:24,176
step by step,

15
00:00:24,176 --> 00:00:25,710
with simple examples,

16
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:27,683
so you can see how each one

17
00:00:27,683 --> 00:00:29,655
makes money, what it costs,

18
00:00:29,655 --> 00:00:32,212
and where people lose their shirts.

19
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:34,699
Three passive income favorites,

20
00:00:34,699 --> 00:00:37,182
three very different money machines.

21
00:00:37,362 --> 00:00:39,319
Vending, car washes,

22
00:00:39,319 --> 00:00:40,102
storage.

23
00:00:40,453 --> 00:00:42,856
Here's an important warning before we start.

24
00:00:43,036 --> 00:00:45,193
Every number in this video is a

25
00:00:45,193 --> 00:00:48,324
simplified illustration we built to teach how

26
00:00:48,324 --> 00:00:49,854
these businesses work,

27
00:00:49,854 --> 00:00:51,524
not an industry average,

28
00:00:51,524 --> 00:00:52,986
a quote or a promise.

29
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:56,246
Real results vary enormously by location,

30
00:00:56,246 --> 00:00:59,777
competition, prices, equipment and how well the

31
00:00:59,777 --> 00:01:00,979
business is run.

32
00:01:01,159 --> 00:01:03,886
Use these examples to learn what questions

33
00:01:03,886 --> 00:01:06,223
to ask, then get real numbers before

34
00:01:06,223 --> 00:01:07,521
you invest a dollar.

35
00:01:07,872 --> 00:01:09,011
For each business,

36
00:01:09,011 --> 00:01:11,227
we'll look at the same five things.

37
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:13,032
What it costs to get in.

38
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:14,582
Where the revenue comes from.

39
00:01:14,762 --> 00:01:16,028
Where the money goes.

40
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:17,868
What's left for the owner.

41
00:01:18,048 --> 00:01:20,331
And the risks that the passive income

42
00:01:20,331 --> 00:01:21,566
videos usually skip.

43
00:01:21,746 --> 00:01:23,769
That way, you can compare all three

44
00:01:23,769 --> 00:01:24,520
side by side.

45
00:01:24,871 --> 00:01:26,662
After the three deep dives,

46
00:01:26,662 --> 00:01:28,785
we'll step back and look at them

47
00:01:28,785 --> 00:01:29,979
from three angles.

48
00:01:30,159 --> 00:01:32,741
The numbers, meaning how the three compare

49
00:01:32,741 --> 00:01:33,909
on return and risk.

50
00:01:34,089 --> 00:01:37,367
The psychology, meaning why passive income is

51
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:39,917
such a powerful and dangerous idea.

52
00:01:40,097 --> 00:01:41,133
And the playbook,

53
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:43,328
meaning how to decide whether any of

54
00:01:43,328 --> 00:01:44,242
these fits you.

55
00:01:44,592 --> 00:01:46,978
Let's start with the smallest and cheapest

56
00:01:46,978 --> 00:01:47,716
of the three:

57
00:01:47,716 --> 00:01:48,852
the vending machine.

58
00:01:49,203 --> 00:01:51,495
All business figures in this video are

59
00:01:51,495 --> 00:01:54,148
simplified illustrations built for teaching.

60
00:01:54,328 --> 00:01:56,616
Lending information comes from the U.

61
00:01:56,796 --> 00:01:57,190
S.

62
00:01:57,370 --> 00:01:59,472
Small Business Administration.

63
00:01:59,652 --> 00:02:03,186
Market return comparisons use an illustrative seven

64
00:02:03,186 --> 00:02:05,264
percent average yearly return,

65
00:02:05,264 --> 00:02:06,235
not a promise.

66
00:02:06,415 --> 00:02:08,239
This video is education,

67
00:02:08,239 --> 00:02:11,279
not financial, legal or business advice.

68
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:12,291
Part one.

69
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:13,679
Vending machines.

70
00:02:13,859 --> 00:02:16,156
The cheapest way into the passive income

71
00:02:16,156 --> 00:02:18,108
dream, and the most misunderstood.

72
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:20,095
Example vending machine.

73
00:02:20,275 --> 00:02:22,817
Cost about four thousand dollars used.

74
00:02:22,997 --> 00:02:25,238
Sales about four hundred dollars a month.

75
00:02:25,418 --> 00:02:27,669
Profit after product and commission,

76
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:30,171
about one hundred forty dollars a month,

77
00:02:30,171 --> 00:02:31,234
before your time.

78
00:02:31,584 --> 00:02:32,722
Cost to get in.

79
00:02:32,902 --> 00:02:35,203
A used snack or drink machine might

80
00:02:35,203 --> 00:02:37,043
cost a few thousand dollars,

81
00:02:37,043 --> 00:02:39,672
while new machines with card readers can

82
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:40,658
cost much more.

83
00:02:40,838 --> 00:02:43,591
Let's say our example machine costs four

84
00:02:43,591 --> 00:02:46,689
thousand dollars, including a card reader and

85
00:02:46,689 --> 00:02:47,653
initial stock.

86
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:49,944
The machine is placed in a small

87
00:02:49,944 --> 00:02:52,187
business with steady foot traffic,

88
00:02:52,187 --> 00:02:54,231
like an auto repair shop with a

89
00:02:54,231 --> 00:02:55,089
waiting room.

90
00:02:55,439 --> 00:02:56,031
Revenue.

91
00:02:56,211 --> 00:02:58,677
Say the machine sells about one hundred

92
00:02:58,677 --> 00:03:00,764
sixty items a month at an average

93
00:03:00,764 --> 00:03:02,724
of two dollars and fifty cents.

94
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:05,036
That's about four hundred dollars a month

95
00:03:05,036 --> 00:03:05,504
in sales.

96
00:03:05,684 --> 00:03:08,416
Some machines in great locations sell far

97
00:03:08,416 --> 00:03:08,749
more.

98
00:03:08,929 --> 00:03:11,611
Many in weak locations sell far less.

99
00:03:11,791 --> 00:03:14,369
Location is the single biggest factor.

100
00:03:14,719 --> 00:03:15,879
Where the money goes.

101
00:03:16,059 --> 00:03:18,564
Product costs might take around forty to

102
00:03:18,564 --> 00:03:20,005
fifty percent of sales,

103
00:03:20,005 --> 00:03:21,946
say one hundred eighty dollars.

104
00:03:22,126 --> 00:03:24,695
Many locations expect a commission,

105
00:03:24,695 --> 00:03:25,870
say ten percent,

106
00:03:25,870 --> 00:03:27,118
or forty dollars.

107
00:03:27,298 --> 00:03:29,471
Card reader fees and the monthly service

108
00:03:29,471 --> 00:03:31,861
might take another twenty to thirty dollars.

109
00:03:32,041 --> 00:03:34,093
That leaves roughly one hundred forty to

110
00:03:34,093 --> 00:03:35,837
one hundred fifty dollars a month.

111
00:03:36,187 --> 00:03:36,989
What's left.

112
00:03:37,169 --> 00:03:39,448
About one hundred forty dollars a month,

113
00:03:39,448 --> 00:03:41,898
or around seventeen hundred dollars a year,

114
00:03:41,898 --> 00:03:43,949
from a four thousand dollar machine.

115
00:03:44,129 --> 00:03:46,048
That looks like a strong return,

116
00:03:46,048 --> 00:03:47,728
around forty percent a year.

117
00:03:47,908 --> 00:03:49,701
But it's before your time,

118
00:03:49,701 --> 00:03:50,943
your gas, repairs,

119
00:03:50,943 --> 00:03:54,184
expired products and the occasional broken coin

120
00:03:54,184 --> 00:03:54,874
mechanism.

121
00:03:55,223 --> 00:03:56,779
Now let's count your time.

122
00:03:56,959 --> 00:03:59,865
Say each machine needs one restocking visit

123
00:03:59,865 --> 00:04:02,501
a week, about an hour including driving

124
00:04:02,501 --> 00:04:03,380
and shopping.

125
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,722
That's around fifty hours a year for

126
00:04:05,722 --> 00:04:07,284
seventeen hundred dollars,

127
00:04:07,284 --> 00:04:09,446
or about thirty four dollars an hour

128
00:04:09,446 --> 00:04:10,827
before gas and repairs.

129
00:04:11,007 --> 00:04:13,562
Not bad, but notice it's not passive.

130
00:04:13,742 --> 00:04:15,567
It's a small part time job with

131
00:04:15,567 --> 00:04:16,156
equipment.

132
00:04:16,506 --> 00:04:17,505
The route effect.

133
00:04:17,685 --> 00:04:19,823
The math improves when you have many

134
00:04:19,823 --> 00:04:21,249
machines close together.

135
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:23,864
Ten machines along one route might take

136
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:25,363
a day a week to service,

137
00:04:25,363 --> 00:04:27,361
and you can buy products in bulk

138
00:04:27,361 --> 00:04:28,360
at lower prices.

139
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:31,357
That's how vending operators turn a hobby

140
00:04:31,357 --> 00:04:32,456
into a business:

141
00:04:32,456 --> 00:04:34,173
by building dense routes,

142
00:04:34,173 --> 00:04:35,959
not by owning one machine.

143
00:04:36,309 --> 00:04:37,192
The risks.

144
00:04:37,372 --> 00:04:39,739
Bad locations, theft and vandalism,

145
00:04:39,739 --> 00:04:41,294
machines breaking down,

146
00:04:41,294 --> 00:04:44,405
products expiring, and locations asking you to

147
00:04:44,405 --> 00:04:47,043
remove your machine with little notice.

148
00:04:47,223 --> 00:04:50,447
Watch out for companies selling expensive machines

149
00:04:50,447 --> 00:04:51,995
with promised locations.

150
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:54,392
Some of those deals put machines in

151
00:04:54,392 --> 00:04:56,482
places that barely sell anything.

152
00:04:56,831 --> 00:04:58,612
Here's how a route might grow,

153
00:04:58,612 --> 00:04:59,978
using our illustration.

154
00:05:00,158 --> 00:05:02,825
Year one, you buy three used machines

155
00:05:02,825 --> 00:05:05,708
with savings, learn which products sell,

156
00:05:05,708 --> 00:05:08,015
and move one machine from a slow

157
00:05:08,015 --> 00:05:09,817
location to a busier one.

158
00:05:09,997 --> 00:05:11,431
By the end of the year,

159
00:05:11,431 --> 00:05:14,174
the three machines earn around five thousand

160
00:05:14,174 --> 00:05:15,233
dollars together.

161
00:05:15,413 --> 00:05:17,975
Year two, you reinvest every dollar of

162
00:05:17,975 --> 00:05:20,267
profit and buy four more machines,

163
00:05:20,267 --> 00:05:21,885
placing them in offices,

164
00:05:21,885 --> 00:05:24,784
gyms and apartment buildings along the same

165
00:05:24,784 --> 00:05:25,189
roads.

166
00:05:25,538 --> 00:05:27,963
Year three, you have around twelve machines

167
00:05:27,963 --> 00:05:29,597
on a route you can service in

168
00:05:29,597 --> 00:05:31,120
about a day and a half each

169
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:31,401
week.

170
00:05:31,581 --> 00:05:34,051
Buying products in bulk from a wholesale

171
00:05:34,051 --> 00:05:35,471
club lowers your costs.

172
00:05:35,651 --> 00:05:37,370
Card readers raise sales,

173
00:05:37,370 --> 00:05:40,190
because many people no longer carry cash.

174
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:41,317
At this point,

175
00:05:41,317 --> 00:05:44,223
the route might earn around twenty thousand

176
00:05:44,223 --> 00:05:46,656
dollars a year before vehicle costs,

177
00:05:46,656 --> 00:05:48,954
which is a meaningful side income,

178
00:05:48,954 --> 00:05:50,982
though still far from passive.

179
00:05:51,331 --> 00:05:53,781
How do operators find good locations?

180
00:05:53,961 --> 00:05:55,587
They walk in and ask.

181
00:05:55,767 --> 00:05:57,646
Auto shops, tire stores,

182
00:05:57,646 --> 00:06:00,543
small factories, apartment buildings,

183
00:06:00,543 --> 00:06:03,518
hotels, gyms and offices with fifty or

184
00:06:03,518 --> 00:06:06,181
more employees are common targets.

185
00:06:06,361 --> 00:06:09,140
The best locations have a captive audience,

186
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:11,660
people who are stuck waiting or working

187
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,116
nearby without easy access to a store.

188
00:06:14,296 --> 00:06:17,464
Some operators offer no commission at first

189
00:06:17,464 --> 00:06:19,748
and add one only once sales are

190
00:06:19,748 --> 00:06:20,264
proven.

191
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:22,401
Vending also has niches.

192
00:06:22,581 --> 00:06:25,622
Micro markets are small self checkout stores

193
00:06:25,622 --> 00:06:28,525
inside offices, with fresh food and higher

194
00:06:28,525 --> 00:06:29,838
sales per location.

195
00:06:30,018 --> 00:06:32,358
Healthy vending focuses on better for you

196
00:06:32,358 --> 00:06:32,758
snacks.

197
00:06:32,938 --> 00:06:35,847
Coffee and specialty machines can earn more

198
00:06:35,847 --> 00:06:36,455
per sale.

199
00:06:36,635 --> 00:06:39,439
Each niche needs different equipment and skills,

200
00:06:39,439 --> 00:06:41,308
but the core lesson is the same:

201
00:06:41,308 --> 00:06:43,294
put the right products in front of

202
00:06:43,294 --> 00:06:44,228
the right crowd.

203
00:06:44,578 --> 00:06:46,566
What does a typical service day look

204
00:06:46,566 --> 00:06:46,842
like?

205
00:06:47,022 --> 00:06:49,515
You load the car with products from

206
00:06:49,515 --> 00:06:50,725
a wholesale club,

207
00:06:50,725 --> 00:06:51,864
drive the route,

208
00:06:51,864 --> 00:06:53,360
restock each machine,

209
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:55,852
rotate older products to the front,

210
00:06:55,852 --> 00:06:57,561
remove anything expired,

211
00:06:57,561 --> 00:07:00,124
collect cash, check the card reader,

212
00:07:00,124 --> 00:07:01,477
and note what sold.

213
00:07:01,657 --> 00:07:04,302
Back home, you update a simple spreadsheet

214
00:07:04,302 --> 00:07:06,822
so you know which products and locations

215
00:07:06,822 --> 00:07:07,578
are winning.

216
00:07:07,758 --> 00:07:09,875
The operators who track this data are

217
00:07:09,875 --> 00:07:10,904
the ones who grow.

218
00:07:11,254 --> 00:07:13,822
One more vending detail that matters:

219
00:07:13,822 --> 00:07:14,378
pricing.

220
00:07:14,558 --> 00:07:17,945
Many operators haven't raised prices in years,

221
00:07:17,945 --> 00:07:19,933
even as product costs rose.

222
00:07:20,113 --> 00:07:22,260
A twenty five cent increase on a

223
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:24,274
two dollar fifty item is a ten

224
00:07:24,274 --> 00:07:25,817
percent price increase,

225
00:07:25,817 --> 00:07:27,629
and if sales barely change,

226
00:07:27,629 --> 00:07:30,179
most of that drops straight to profit.

227
00:07:30,359 --> 00:07:32,166
Card readers help here too,

228
00:07:32,166 --> 00:07:34,977
because customers paying by card are often

229
00:07:34,977 --> 00:07:37,788
less sensitive to small price changes than

230
00:07:37,788 --> 00:07:39,194
those counting coins.

231
00:07:39,543 --> 00:07:41,006
The verdict on vending.

232
00:07:41,186 --> 00:07:43,332
It's a low cost way to learn

233
00:07:43,332 --> 00:07:45,324
business basics: location,

234
00:07:45,324 --> 00:07:48,466
pricing, inventory and customer behavior.

235
00:07:48,646 --> 00:07:50,962
One machine won't change your life,

236
00:07:50,962 --> 00:07:53,013
but a well run route can become

237
00:07:53,013 --> 00:07:54,999
a real side business or even a

238
00:07:54,999 --> 00:07:55,925
full time one.

239
00:07:56,105 --> 00:07:58,506
Just don't believe anyone who calls it

240
00:07:58,506 --> 00:07:59,391
truly passive.

241
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:00,483
Part two.

242
00:08:00,663 --> 00:08:01,639
Car washes.

243
00:08:01,819 --> 00:08:03,535
A big step up in money,

244
00:08:03,535 --> 00:08:05,476
and a big step up in risk.

245
00:08:05,825 --> 00:08:08,043
Example express car wash.

246
00:08:08,223 --> 00:08:10,959
Revenue about one point two million dollars

247
00:08:10,959 --> 00:08:11,404
a year.

248
00:08:11,584 --> 00:08:14,234
Owner earnings about three hundred thousand dollars

249
00:08:14,234 --> 00:08:14,858
before debt.

250
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:17,732
Cost to build several million dollars.

251
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:19,874
There are a few kinds of car

252
00:08:19,874 --> 00:08:20,322
washes.

253
00:08:20,502 --> 00:08:23,471
Self service bays where customers wash their

254
00:08:23,471 --> 00:08:24,078
own cars.

255
00:08:24,258 --> 00:08:25,773
In bay automatics,

256
00:08:25,773 --> 00:08:27,625
often at gas stations.

257
00:08:27,805 --> 00:08:29,054
And express tunnels,

258
00:08:29,054 --> 00:08:31,676
where cars ride a conveyor through brushes

259
00:08:31,676 --> 00:08:33,424
and dryers in a few minutes.

260
00:08:33,604 --> 00:08:36,645
Express tunnels have become very popular with

261
00:08:36,645 --> 00:08:40,024
investors, largely because of monthly memberships.

262
00:08:40,374 --> 00:08:41,617
Cost to get in.

263
00:08:41,797 --> 00:08:44,308
Building a new express tunnel can cost

264
00:08:44,308 --> 00:08:45,895
several million dollars,

265
00:08:45,895 --> 00:08:48,472
including land, building and equipment.

266
00:08:48,652 --> 00:08:51,137
Buying an existing wash may cost less

267
00:08:51,137 --> 00:08:53,622
or more depending on its performance.

268
00:08:53,802 --> 00:08:56,773
Smaller self service washes can be cheaper,

269
00:08:56,773 --> 00:08:59,467
sometimes in the hundreds of thousands.

270
00:08:59,647 --> 00:09:01,505
This is not a small purchase.

271
00:09:01,854 --> 00:09:02,516
Revenue.

272
00:09:02,696 --> 00:09:04,740
The magic of the modern car wash

273
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:05,889
is the membership.

274
00:09:06,069 --> 00:09:08,104
Customers pay a monthly fee,

275
00:09:08,104 --> 00:09:09,848
say twenty five dollars,

276
00:09:09,848 --> 00:09:11,375
for unlimited washes.

277
00:09:11,555 --> 00:09:14,169
Say our example wash has two thousand

278
00:09:14,169 --> 00:09:15,653
five hundred members.

279
00:09:15,833 --> 00:09:18,885
That's sixty two thousand five hundred dollars

280
00:09:18,885 --> 00:09:21,539
a month, or seven hundred fifty thousand

281
00:09:21,539 --> 00:09:24,126
a year, before a single individual wash

282
00:09:24,126 --> 00:09:24,657
is sold.

283
00:09:24,837 --> 00:09:26,356
Add individual washes,

284
00:09:26,356 --> 00:09:29,186
and total revenue reaches about one point

285
00:09:29,186 --> 00:09:30,015
two million.

286
00:09:30,364 --> 00:09:31,595
Where the money goes.

287
00:09:31,775 --> 00:09:33,593
Labor, even with automation,

288
00:09:33,593 --> 00:09:36,384
might take around twenty to thirty percent.

